"""Flashcard format (hint + the math + airplane translation + breakdown): CMO topics 05-07."""
B = {
501: ("Which way should the nose point if the radar only tells you the slope under your wheels?",
  [r"<strong>$\nabla f(x_k)$ is the radar.</strong> It points up the steepest slope, so the minus sign swings the nose 180° to point straight downhill.",
   r"<strong>$\alpha_k$ is the flight distance.</strong> Multiplying by it turns a direction into an actual displacement: how far you fly before checking the radar again.",
   r"<strong>Why stop at $\|g_k\|\le\text{tol}\,\|g_0\|$?</strong> Flat ground is never exactly 0.0000, so the landing check compares the slope now with the cliff you started on."]),
508: ("What tells the pilot when to stop flying straight and turn, and why is the turn exactly 90°?",
  [r"<strong>Numerator $-g^\top u$ = downward momentum.</strong> How steeply the heading $u$ dives down the slope: a steeper dive means a longer leg.",
   r"<strong>Denominator $u^\top Qu$ = the brakes.</strong> How fast the terrain curves back up along $u$: a sharper curve means you must stop sooner.",
   r"<strong>Momentum over brakes = the distance to the bottom of that straight track.</strong> Right there the new slope is perpendicular to $u$, which is why every turn is 90° and the path zig-zags."]),
502: ("Why can the pilot fly legs of exactly $1/L$ without ever overshooting the valley?",
  [r"<strong>$L$ = the sharpest bend anywhere.</strong> The ground can never curve back up faster than $L$, so a leg of $1/L$ is a safe distance everywhere.",
   r"<strong>$\|x_0-x^*\|^2$ = how far away you start.</strong> A bigger first cliff means a bigger gap to close.",
   r"<strong>Dividing by $2k$:</strong> each extra leg trims only a shrinking share of what is left, so the error falls like $1/k$ (sublinear), not geometrically."]),
503: ("By what fixed fraction does each leg shrink the distance to the valley floor?",
  [r"<strong>$\kappa=L/\mu$ = sharpest bend over gentlest bend.</strong> It measures how stretched the valley is.",
   r"<strong>$\frac{\kappa-1}{\kappa+1}$ = the contraction per leg.</strong> A round bowl ($\kappa=1$) gives 0 (one leg); a long thin canyon ($\kappa$ large) gives almost 1 (almost no progress).",
   r"<strong>The power $k$:</strong> the same fraction is multiplied in again on every leg, so the error decays geometrically (linear convergence)."]),
504: ("How badly can the nose be misaligned with the valley axis, and what does it cost per leg?",
  [r"<strong>The ratio $\frac{(x^\top x)^2}{(x^\top Qx)(x^\top Q^{-1}x)}$</strong> compares straight downhill with the valley's two extreme curvatures; it equals 1 when the nose is perfectly aligned.",
   r"<strong>$\frac{4Aa}{(A+a)^2}$ = the worst case.</strong> It is set by the sharpest curvature $A$ and the gentlest curvature $a$.",
   r"<strong>Result:</strong> each leg shrinks the altitude gap above the floor by at least $\left(\frac{\kappa-1}{\kappa+1}\right)^2$, the square of the distance rate."]),
505: ("Why does a very long, thin canyon make the pilot zig-zag instead of flying down the middle?",
  [r"<strong>$\lambda_{\max}$ = the steep canyon wall, $\lambda_{\min}$ = the gentle floor.</strong> Their ratio $\kappa$ says how stretched the valley is.",
   r"<strong>The radar mostly sees the steep wall,</strong> so the nose points across the canyon and each leg slams into the opposite side.",
   r"<strong>As $\kappa\to\infty$ the factor $\frac{\kappa-1}{\kappa+1}\to1$:</strong> each leg barely shrinks the error, which is the stagnation."]),
506: ("How can the pilot size the next leg from the last two footprints with no radar sweep?",
  [r"<strong>$s_k$ = how far you flew; $y_k$ = how much the slope reading changed.</strong> Together they say how fast the ground curves along your path.",
   r"<strong>BB1 $=s^\top s/s^\top y$</strong> is distance squared over slope change: one over the local curvature, which is the bottom-of-the-curve distance.",
   r"<strong>BB2 $=s^\top y/y^\top y$</strong> is the same idea measured from the slope side. Neither needs a line search."]),
507: ("What lets a heavy plane keep rolling down the canyon instead of bouncing off the walls?",
  [r"<strong>$-\alpha\nabla f(x_k)$</strong> is the usual radar-guided push downhill.",
   r"<strong>$\beta(x_k-x_{k-1})$ = momentum.</strong> The last leg's displacement carries you on, so the side-to-side bounces cancel while the steady downhill part adds up.",
   r"<strong>The rate $\frac{\sqrt\kappa-1}{\sqrt\kappa+1}$</strong> uses $\sqrt\kappa$ instead of $\kappa$, so thin canyons cost far fewer legs."]),
601: ("What shape does the autopilot fit to the terrain, and where does it fly?",
  [r"<strong>$\nabla^2f$ = the radar that sees curvature,</strong> not only the slope but how fast the slope changes.",
   r"<strong>Solving $\nabla^2f\,p=-\nabla f$</strong> flies to the bottom of the parabola built from slope and curvature.",
   r"<strong>The inverse Hessian rescales the nose:</strong> steep walls get short moves, flat floors long moves, so the zig-zag disappears."]),
602: ("What happens to the error once you are inside the terminal approach bubble?",
  [r"<strong>$\|x_k-x^*\|^2$:</strong> the error is squared each leg, so $0.1\to0.01\to0.0001$ and the number of correct digits doubles.",
   r"<strong>$\frac{L}{2m}$ = roughest bend over gentlest bend</strong> controls how small the bubble is: the bigger the ratio, the closer you must start.",
   r"<strong>Superlinear:</strong> the ratio of successive errors tends to 0, faster than any fixed geometric rate."]),
603: ("What goes wrong when the radar sees the ground curving downward?",
  [r"<strong>If $\nabla^2f$ is not positive definite,</strong> the fitted parabola opens downward or is flat, and its 'bottom' is really a peak or a saddle.",
   r"<strong>The Newton nose $p_k$ then need not satisfy $p_k^\top\nabla f\lt0$:</strong> it can point uphill.",
   r"<strong>Fix:</strong> check the curvature first and stabilise (Levenberg-Marquardt) or fall back to straight downhill when it is not all positive."]),
604: ("When does the pilot take the full unit step and when only a cautious one?",
  [r"<strong>$\alpha_k=\beta^m$:</strong> start with the full step 1 and multiply by $\beta$ until the Armijo altitude drop is achieved.",
   r"<strong>$c_1\lt\tfrac12$</strong> is a modest demand for altitude loss, so near the optimum the full Newton step already satisfies it.",
   r"<strong>Result:</strong> short careful legs far away and full Newton legs ($\alpha=1$) near the valley, which keeps the fast local rate."]),
605: ("How does the pilot make a hill-shaped terrain model safe to fly to?",
  [r"<strong>$\tau_kI$ adds stiffness along every axis,</strong> lifting all the curvatures by $\tau_k$.",
   r"<strong>$\tau_k\ge-\lambda_{\min}+\delta$</strong> is just enough that even the most negative curvature becomes strictly positive.",
   r"<strong>The dial:</strong> $\tau\to0$ is pure Newton (trust the curvature), $\tau\to\infty$ is a tiny gradient step (trust only the slope)."]),
606: ("Why does Newton cross the banana canyon in one leg while gradient descent switchbacks?",
  [r"<strong>$H=\operatorname{diag}(2,200)$:</strong> the curvature along $x_2$ is 100 times that along $x_1$ (steep walls, gentle floor), so $\kappa=100$.",
   r"<strong>$p_N=-H^{-1}g$ divides each slope component by its own curvature,</strong> giving the move $(1,0)$ straight down the floor.",
   r"<strong>Gradient descent sees only the slope,</strong> so with $\kappa=100$ it zig-zags between the walls."]),
701: ("Why are Q-conjugate headings better than perpendicular ones in a skewed valley?",
  [r"<strong>$d_i^\top Qd_j=0$ = a $Q$-twisted right angle.</strong> The valley's own curvature $Q$ decides what counts as 'not interfering', so moving along $d_j$ does not undo the altitude already won along $d_i$.",
   r"<strong>$\alpha_k=-g_k^\top d_k/d_k^\top Qd_k$:</strong> downward momentum over the brakes along that heading, exactly the bottom of that line.",
   r"<strong>$r_k=-g_k$</strong> is the residual: the same radar reading with the sign flipped."]),
702: ("After k legs, where in the whole flight plane is the pilot sitting?",
  [r"<strong>$x_k=\arg\min_{x_0+\mathcal B_k}f$:</strong> you are at the lowest point of the entire flight plane spanned by all $k$ headings so far, not just along the last line.",
   r"<strong>$g_k^\top d_i=0$ for every earlier heading:</strong> the slope under you is perpendicular to all of them, so no earlier heading can improve the altitude any more.",
   r"<strong>Why it matters:</strong> later conjugate legs never spoil what is already won."]),
703: ("Which fraction of the old heading does the pilot keep when choosing the next one?",
  [r"<strong>$-g_{k+1}$ is the fresh downhill from the radar;</strong> $\beta_kd_k$ is the memory of the previous flight (the GPS memory).",
   r"<strong>$\beta_k=\|g_{k+1}\|^2/\|g_k\|^2$:</strong> if the slope has shrunk a lot, you keep little of the old heading; if not, you keep more.",
   r"<strong>$\alpha_k=\|r_k\|^2/d_k^\top Qd_k$:</strong> the leg length is still momentum over brakes, along the remembered heading."]),
704: ("How many legs does the pilot need, and what do the legs span?",
  [r"<strong>$\mathcal K_k=\operatorname{span}\{r_0,Qr_0,\dots\}$</strong> is the flight plane the radar and the curvature gauge can reach in $k$ legs.",
   r"<strong>Each leg kills one more natural axis of the valley,</strong> so with $m$ distinct curvature values you land after at most $m\le n$ legs.",
   r"<strong>Why it matters:</strong> in exact arithmetic CG is a direct method, and an iterative one that is usually done long before $n$ legs."]),
705: ("What stops the pilot following a corrupted heading when the terrain is not a quadratic?",
  [r"<strong>$\beta^{PR}=g_{k+1}^\top(g_{k+1}-g_k)/\|g_k\|^2$</strong> measures how much the radar reading actually changed, instead of only its size.",
   r"<strong>$\beta^+=\max(0,\beta^{PR})$:</strong> if the formula goes negative, the memory is thrown away and the pilot restarts with straight downhill.",
   r"<strong>Why it matters:</strong> restarting protects the global convergence of non-linear CG."]),
706: ("Why does the second leg finish the job in 2D?",
  [r"<strong>Leg 1 ($\alpha_0=1/5$):</strong> fly straight downhill from $x_0$ until the altitude stops falling, landing at $x_1$.",
   r"<strong>$\beta_0=0.36$ blends the memory in:</strong> the second heading is fresh downhill plus 36% of the first heading, which makes it $Q$-conjugate to it.",
   r"<strong>Leg 2 ($\alpha_1=5/16$):</strong> flying that heading removes the remaining error, so $x_2=x^*=(0,0)$ exactly: two legs for two dimensions."]),
}
FIX = {cid: {"hint": h, "breakdown": b} for cid, (h, b) in B.items()}
